# Never run a build inside the tool sandbox

Bundler and framework builds hang **indefinitely** in the agent's tool sandbox.
Always pass `required_permissions: ["all"]` when running one.

```
# ❌ hangs forever — killed at 16 minutes, no output, dist/ never written
Shell(command: "npm run build")

# ✅ the same build, 1.6 seconds
Shell(command: "npm run build", required_permissions: ["all"])
```

## What this covers

Anything that invokes a bundler, directly or as a step inside another script:

- `npm run build` — rollup, Next.js, vite, tsup
- `npm run test:bundle`, `npm run release`, `npm run build-storybook`
- `npx rollup -c`, `npx next build`

## What is fine sandboxed

`npm run test` (vitest), `npm run lint` (eslint), `npm run typecheck` (tsc),
`git`, `node -e`, file listing. These run at full speed — seconds, not minutes.
Keep using the sandbox for them.

## Recognising it

The build prints its first line and then nothing at all. `dist/` keeps its old
timestamps. There is no error, no crash and no timeout — it sits there until
you kill it, so a long wait never resolves into a useful failure.

Do **not** pipe a build through `tail` or `head`. That buffers every line, so a
stalled build looks exactly like a running one and you lose the only signal you
had. Let build output stream.

## Measured in source-components-react, August 2026

| | Sandboxed | `required_permissions: ["all"]` |
|---|---|---|
| `npm run build` | killed at 16 min, stalled right after `index.ts → dist/index.cjs...` | 1.6 s, all four bundles |

Reproduced deliberately a second time with `npx rollup -c`: still frozen at that
same line after 4m45s, then killed. It does not eventually recover.

It is **not** `worker_threads` — a worker spawns in 12 ms sandboxed. The cause
is unidentified, so treat every build as affected rather than guessing which
bundler is safe. Asking for `["all"]` costs nothing; guessing wrong costs the
session.
